printFormatT -- SEQ 449 R4 (2026-10-28): the PRINTXp reshape -- PRINTXp is FORMAt; SHORTCUt; EXPRESSIOn;, and a format
is an item of its own.

This file is not run alone. pop.sh builds one input per row: everything above this file's `stop();`, then the row's
statements (` @@ ` splits lines), then a sentinel on stderr. One process per row; the row's STDOUT, less the run's noise
lines, newlines read as `|`, is what is compared.

THE RULE (Tony, SEQ 449 R4; the design in his offline note of 2026-10-28): a format item sets the implicit format for
ITS data type -- the type its letter names (d i u x X o c: a count; f e E g G: a number; s: a string or token) -- for
every later item of that type, to the end of its print statement. Another format for the same type replaces it. Items
of other types keep their own format. The defaults (%d, %.1f, %s) come back at the next print.

THE OLD ROAD IS NOT THE ORACLE HERE. On both roads, before SEQ 449 R1, a `#` after an expression was taken as a STRINGXp:
the old road prints `print qa #5d:;` as `2 5 d`. A format after an expression does not apply there in today's tree, so these rows pin
the INTENDED value (Tony, SEQ 449 R4), and no -o row exists.

BORN RED on trunk (S0), each row's today measured on e0f8e0c0: a print that starts with a format builds no items at
all and prints nothing; `qa #5d qb` prints `2 5` (FORMAt is only tried after an EXPRESSIOn under PRINTFIELd, and its
`5` survives as a print item).

